r B1 with something? B1 is a single. Maybe not. So maybe only one chow. Then, with pairs. The pair of J1. If I can form another pair, that would help. But I need to have a pair. So maybe discard a tile that's not part of a pair.\n\nSo, if I discard T6, which is a single, that leaves me with more potential for pairs. For example, J1 is a pair. If I can get another J1, that would be a pung. But I have two already. So maybe not. Alternatively, if I discard W4, keeping one, then W4 could be part of a pair. But I need another one.\n\nHmm. This is tricky. Let's think about the valid actions again. The options are: Play W4; Play B6; Play B1; Play W7; Play B5; Play J2; Play B8; Play J1; Play W3; Play F3; Play B7; Play T6.\n\nDiscarding B6, B5, B7 would break the chow, so avoid those. So remaining options are W4, B1, W7, J2, B8, J1, W3, F3, T6.\n\nAmong these, which is the best to discard. Let's think about the possibility of forming more melds. For example, if I discard J2, maybe someone else can use it. But I have a pair of J1, which is better to keep. So maybe discard J2. If I discard J2, then my hand has J1 (2), which is a pair. That's good. Then, I can focus on forming other melds. For example, the B5-B6-B7 chow. Also, if I can form a chow in W tiles. But I need W5. Not sure.\n\nAlternatively, discard F3. Since it's a single, and not part of any meld. That's also a possibility. But F3 is West Wind. Maybe someone else needs it for a pung. But I don't know.\n\nAnother angle: which tile has the highest chance of being safe to discard. For example, B8 is in my hand. The unshown count for B8 is 3. So if I discard B8, there are three left. Maybe someone else has a B8 and can use it. But I have B8 in my hand. If I discard it, I lose that tile. But if I keep it, maybe it can form a pair or part of a chow. For example, B8 is a terminal. If I have B8 and another B8, that's a pair. But I have only one. So not helpful. So maybe discard B8 is safe.\n\nWait, the valid actions include